      "latency_ms": 8143.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1157,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (a property called **cohesion**) and to other substances (a property called **adhesion**).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter on Earth:\n*   **Solid:** As ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** As the water we drink and bathe in.\n*   **Gas:** As water vapor or steam.\n\nThis ability to change states easily is the driving force behind the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, and precipitation).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport medium in the human body and in nature (carrying nutrients and minerals).\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on ponds without sinking.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat energy without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the human body from overheating.\n\n### 4. Abundance and Importance\nWater covers about 71% of the Earth's surface, mostly in the oceans. It is the only substance found naturally on Earth in all three states and is essential for life as we know it. All known forms of life require water to survive."
